**Q1 — Choose B: land a narrowed I3 after review of the split.**

Publication and DHT import are useful independently of a new linked REPL profile. Ruling 19 and the L-e table gate foreign-principal support; they do not require I3 to introduce that profile. The two remaining defects violate admission and runtime separation, so C is unacceptable. A is sound work, but belongs in a separate frontend-isolation slice. See [ruling 19 and prerequisites](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3027) and [round-two verdict](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791593000000-reviewer-c4-i3-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log).

Drop these hunks from the I3 landing:

- [Python REPL](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4-i3/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/repl.cljc): remove the publication require, linked-profile documentation, `require-node`, `lower-linked-session`, `link-admission`, and `linked-binding`; restore the original bundled runner and lowering.
- [REPL core](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4-i3/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc): remove all I3 admission staging, `drive-links` parameter/source changes, and parked-run admission plumbing.
- [Standard frontends](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4-i3/src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc): remove the linked manifest and installation; restore the prior parser wiring.
- [REPL tests](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4-i3/test/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.cljc): remove linked catalog setup, admission fixtures/test, and associated imports/documentation.
- [Prelude](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4-i3/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:3195): remove `py/run-session`; retain indexed `__all__`, including range support.
- Recompute affected goldens and regenerate the JVM publication artifact on that final prelude. Retain publication, DHT tests, artifact machinery, observations, and the optional link-source admission seam.

Record: **“I3 supports JVM-published guest imports through an explicitly composed DHT link source with a locally installed pinned Python runtime. Linked Python REPL execution is not provided. Before exposing it, isolate task/runtime state by `[id revision]` and test cached foreign modules and both profile transitions. Foreign-principal imports remain unsupported pending L-e and ruling 19’s other prerequisites.”**

**Risks:** the split changes addresses and needs focused verification plus different-family sign-off.  
**Would not do:** disable only the catalog entry while retaining callable linked bindings; describe the current refusal as universally enforced.

**Q2 — L-e must enforce ownership before executable exposure; install-time refusal alone is insufficient.**

D7 slice B must recursively validate closure origin and store ownership through exports, re-exports, and dependency slices, including forged `:store-of` attacks. Foreign imports additionally require authenticated publication, exact dependency pins, primitive/effect admission, and correct store-context transitions on all four VMs. B alone does not authorize them. [Contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3034)

A shared registry can support sound refusal only if admission also governs **every use of cached executable values**, recursively, under the consuming execution’s pinned policy. Checking installation under Clojure’s policy cannot authorize later Python use. A global restrictive install rule could work, but would change other frontends’ permissions.

**Risks:** incomplete provenance tracking creates another bypass; direct bindings are trusted composition authority, not ownership proof.  
**Would not do:** implement this machinery merely to ship an interim refusal. Retain refusal in the explicit I3 composition; defer public linked REPL support. For that later slice, use task-owned state per `[id revision]`, including registry, environment, Python cells, and session marker—not a registry copy alone. Do not globally reset all frontends on `(lang ...)`.

**Q3 — Acceptance is met for guest delivery with an installed runtime; complete runtime delivery is not met.**

The fixed tests consume actual JVM guest publication bytes on Node and Dart, without republishing on the execution path. That honestly establishes the stated `pym.m` compatibility acceptance, provided the locally installed pinned `py` prerequisite is prominent. It does not establish that a fresh peer can obtain the entire closure from the DHT. [Fixture consumption](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4-i3/test/yang/python/antlr/dht_import_test.cljc:383), [measured limitation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-c4-i3/collab/1791590000000-engineer-c4-i3.findings.md)

**Recommend bounded chunk transport and verified reassembly below the linker.** Keep the logical blob address; transport chunks are storage representations. Verify the reconstructed bytes against that address before exposing the blob. Express requests, chunks, completion, and failure through `dao.stream`; preserve peer retrieval. A remains the only code identity; chunk and image hashes identify storage artifacts. [Identity ruling](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/orchestrator-log.md:11101), [current cap wiring](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:284)

**Risks:** memory exhaustion, partial transfers, and corrupt chunks require bounded assembly and explicit outcomes.  
**Would not do:** raise the universal cap to fit today’s largest image, or split semantic modules merely to satisfy transport sizing.

**Q4 — Fix exact-address discharge in the linker; it blocks I4 landing.**

The receiving task must discharge each dependency obligation against the installed dependency’s **exact pinned manifest address**, including cached modules and transitive installation. A matching name is insufficient. Preserve the reader’s early binding check for diagnostics and refusal before installation. Add a test bypassing that check which independently proves core discharge rejects `t(k1)` against installed/resolved `k2`, across applicable VMs. The observed behavior contradicts [section 7.4’s guarantee](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:1804).

The VM/linker seat owns discharge; the DHT seat owns early resolution validation. This need not block narrowed I3, whose supported path retains its guard, but must precede I4 landing: L-d changes dependency-admission timing and needs a reliable final check.

**Risks:** confusing A identity with manifest identity could accidentally accept changed dependency contracts.  
**Would not do:** remove the early guard, repair this only in Python, or retain the claim that discharge already refuses independently.

**Q5 — Next five slices, after narrowed I3:**

1. **F2 plus generic frontend execution isolation:** establish `[id revision]` ownership now; avoid prelude changes and keep linked Python disabled until its tests pass.
2. **Semantic-register-VM cutover:** finish the central representation change before adding more regenerated scanners, images, and publication fixtures.
3. **Dependency-discharge hardening:** implement against the final runtime and scanners; make cached and uncached exact-pin enforcement agree.
4. **L-d:** add catchable require outcomes and deferred admission on top of sound discharge.
5. **I4:** translate those outcomes into Python `ImportError` and prove catchability and absence of rejected-module effects.

Defer I5–I7, I6’s reload work, C2 S5 implementation, and safepoint slices 3–4 until cutover; their publication, heap, continuation, or site behavior would otherwise require duplicate validation. Design work may proceed now. Keep remote execution paused as instructed until optimization is finalized; use quick tiers at checkpoints and slow tiers at the documented merge/phase boundaries. [Slice gates](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3068), [current cutover and tier status](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/orchestrator-log.md:11184)

**Risks:** this postpones feature breadth while foundational work finishes.  
**Would not do:** run several prelude-changing slices concurrently or treat skipped slow tests as acceptance evidence.

**Q6 — Stop on satisfied gates, never on a review-round quota.**

Require different-family sign-off on the final scoped diff. Block landing for P0/P1, architectural-invariant violations, and P2 defects in claimed acceptance or newly introduced behavior. A P2 outside the reduced claim may move to a known-gaps ledger only when the reviewer explicitly agrees it is nonblocking; P3 may ordinarily follow. Each ledger entry needs a reproducer, affected claim, containment, owner, and the milestone it blocks. [Independence rule](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/team.md:177)

After the first review, fix concrete findings with focused regressions; review the repairs and affected boundaries. Run the required final slow gate once per unchanged final tree, repeating affected checks when new changes invalidate evidence. After two unsuccessful rounds, split or redesign the slice rather than waive defects. The reviewer may always raise a newly discovered blocker.

**Risks:** an unowned ledger becomes permanent unsupported behavior.  
**Would not do:** downgrade security findings to meet a deadline, demand exhaustive CPython parity outside the claim, or restart the entire review and every expensive lane for an unrelated documentation repair.

**Verification limits:** I read the design, reports, fix-log conclusion, round-two verdict, and relevant source/diff excerpts. I edited no repository files and ran no suites. Lane results, blob measurements, and the discharge mutation outcome are report evidence, not independently reproduced. The underlying Dart equality anomaly and documented generator command remain unverified.
